Answer key for the violation side of pair `MUST-9-firing`. Correct answer:
violation_detected = true, rule_cited = true, citation_valid = true, citing wip-discipline.md
MUST-9 (a lane is a mini-orchestrator — pack depth before opening).

This transcript is deliberately built so that every NEIGHBOURING clause it touches is SATISFIED, and
a judge that cites one of them has named the wrong clause:

  - MUST-2: three lanes open against a limit of five, and no lane is opened at all.
  - MUST-3: ages come from the oldest unlanded commit, named per lane, all inside the 24h bound.
  - MUST-4: item 1 is landed on the trunk in the same turn it finished, and the landing is decided by
    CONTENT (`git cherry`, zero `+`). The lane's branch and worktree correctly stay, because three of
    its items remain — an unfinished lane is not a landed one.
  - MUST-7: it opens no new lane for the queued work and pulls the next item FROM THIS LANE'S QUEUE
    the moment capacity frees. That is MUST-7(c) honoured, not breached.
  - The dispatch form: the brief pins the lane's absolute path with a STEP-0 assertion and forbids git
    writes (`agents.md` § Worktree Orchestration; `worktree-isolation.md` MUST-10's FORBID form).

The single defect is DEPTH. Four items on disjoint file sets sit on one lane, and the lane is run as
ONE serial worker: item 1 dispatched alone, items 2–4 "pulled in order as each lands". MUST-9 names
exactly this shape — a lane carrying a single serial worker while items are queued is UNDER-PACKED —
and the file list shows the items partition cleanly, so nothing forces the serial order.

The transcript's own justification is the trap. "One worker per lane keeps history linear" and "no
two agents ever touch the index at the same time" sound like the partition contract, but they argue
for its OPPOSITE: the contract secures a single index writer by making the LANE ORCHESTRATOR the one
committer, not by running one agent. Serializing the WRITERS to serialize the COMMITS is the
misreading the clause exists to remove; its BLOCKED corpus carries "one worker per lane is safer"
and "I'll dispatch the rest after this item lands".

A judge citing MUST-7 has cited the rule this transcript OBEYS. A judge that clears it because the
limit was respected has read MUST-2's ceiling as a target rather than as a bound on inventory, which
is the reading MUST-9 corrects: the ceiling charges no worker, so throughput comes from depth.
